WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.150
One of the most popular, heartfelt,

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:05.190
and stupid critiques of modern society is that

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:08.250
technology is destroying our civilization.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.950
The plain truth is that technology

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.900
isn't enslaving our minds or killing the planet.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.290
It's an extension of who we are as humans.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:18.770
And massive advances in energy, creation, food production,

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.560
computer programming, and industrialization

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.020
are the reasons why the planet supports billions of people

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.790
who live more prosperously and peacefully,

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.950
and why global life expectancy keeps growing.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:32.720
Humans actually have been getting better

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.470
at a lot of things for a long time.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:38.750
In terms of heading off various diseases,

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:42.110
heading off poverty, and heading off a lot of things.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.530
For the past 60 years, Stewart Brand has been

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.810
one of the biggest champions of experimenting

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:50.460
with technology to solve humanity's biggest problems.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.203
You can't count on the past ways of making it better

00:00:54.203 --> 00:00:56.830
to fix whatever your current problems are.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:58.890
You have to keep discovering new ones.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.010
In his early days, he was one of Ken Kesey's

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.130
Merry Pranksters, using psychedelics,

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.940
music, and light shows to expand consciousness.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.300
By the late 1960s, he started the Whole Earth Catalog,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.390
which quickly became a bible to hippies and techno geeks,

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.680
such as Steve Jobs,

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.870
who famously quoted its parting message.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.620
Stay hungry, stay foolish.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.520
Dubbed the intellectual Johnny Appleseed

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.790
of the counter-culture, Brand's ideas

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.380
and activism helped inspire the first Earth Day.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.320
And his early reporting on the personal computer revolution

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.230
turned machines that were once synonymous

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.520
with centralized, bureaucratic oppression,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.540
into devices of individual empowerment.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.610
His life is the subject of a new documentary film

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.870
that takes its name from a famous Brand mantra.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.280
He had the understanding

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.040
that humans were on a trajectory

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.373
towards greater and technical possibilities.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.270
And he made an important statement.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.963
We are as gods, so we might as well get good at it.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.920
His current passion is Revive &amp; Restore,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.240
an organization that is leading the de-extinction movement

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.590
by using biotechnology to bring back

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.230
extinct plants and animals.

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.290
Including the passenger pigeon, the woolly mammoth,

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:15.250
and the once dominant American chestnut tree.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:20.250
This is what killed 5 billion American chestnut trees.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.840
These trees are functionally extinct

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.340
because they can't reproduce

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.720
and make a forest like they used to.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.540
But genetically they're not extinct at all.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.830
They've got all the variety they ever had.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.600
And they're about to get one more bit of variety,

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.020
which is one or two genes that'll help them

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.170
head off the diseases that we brought here.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:41.223
Not on purpose.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.760
But the cure is on purpose.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.320
As the film shows, Brand's techno-optimism

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.360
isn't without its critics.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.790
I would love to see a mammoth.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.683
Oh, wouldn't that be cool?

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.940
That we can do it is so much fun

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.173
and so fascinating for people who love technology.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:04.539
At the same time we ought to be asking, should we do this?

00:03:04.539 --> 00:03:06.610
Is this a good idea?

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.270
It's a criticism Brand rejects

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.260
throughout the documentary.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.060
When genetically modified organisms were first created,

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.823
most environmentalists were just automatically against it.

00:03:19.571 --> 00:03:21.750
And that actually makes me pretty mad

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.820
because that's taking ideology more seriously

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:28.830
than the ecosystems you're trying to protect.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.710
While Brand's vision for de-extinction

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.930
has struggled with the very environmental movement

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.210
he helped create, he takes solace in

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:38.440
the historic cycles of rejection and acceptance.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.863
When in vitro fertilization first came for humans

00:03:41.863 --> 00:03:44.200
there was all the usual resistances.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.793
Hubris, you're playing God,

00:03:46.663 --> 00:03:50.433
all those in vitro babies are gonna be sick somehow,

00:03:51.471 --> 00:03:53.226
they're gonna be fucked up.

00:03:53.226 --> 00:03:55.730
And as soon as you had a couple of in vitro kids

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:58.420
who are adorable, and healthy,

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.990
and you had ecstatic parents who could

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.950
not otherwise have children, that just flipped.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.810
It just turned off.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:10.300
And so when we clone now a black-footed ferret,

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.030
or Przewalski's horse, people are going,

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.380
oh they're adorable animal.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.210
And you say, well it's cloned, you know.

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:18.430
They went, oh really?

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.107
Can I do that to my pet?

00:04:20.107 --> 00:04:21.850
And it flips.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:24.900
Yeah, it's more about optimism versus pessimism.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.370
It's more about the world you want to live in

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.870
versus the world that you're running from.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.800
The co-directors of "We Are as Gods,"

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.010
David Alvarado and Jason Sussberg,

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:37.530
whose previous work profiled transhumanists

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.520
experimenting with immortality,

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.890
find value in Brand's perspective.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:45.260
Actually what's more interesting is the use of technology,

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.530
the discussion between whether technology

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.890
got us to this problem where we have global warming,

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:54.360
where we have an economic system in ruin.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.439
And whether or not we can use technological tools

00:04:56.439 --> 00:05:00.410
like Bitcoin, or other sort of financial technology

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:03.060
to get us out of the current mini depression that we're in.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:06.480
Or using geo-engineering, or other technologies

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.910
to get us out of climate change.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.753
I think that it's just really easy

00:05:10.753 --> 00:05:13.390
to just say it's technology's fault.

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:14.760
Well, what's the solution here?

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:15.850
What are we talking about?

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:17.140
What is the solution that

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:18.590
that kind of person would be suggesting?

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:19.960
Because I just feel like we need to be

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.930
working on it together and building better tools,

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:24.540
which goes right back to the '60s

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:25.920
and what Stewart was talking about.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.780
It's been a long, strange trip for Stewart Brand

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:31.770
and the planet he helped us recognize from outer space,

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:35.410
by goading NASA into releasing images of the whole Earth.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.230
What has kept him on the forefront of what comes next,

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:40.170
from psychedelics, to the internet,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.860
to reviving extinct species, is his undying belief

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.240
that technological progress will help us

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.780
triumph over our largest problems.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.580
Now in his 80s, Brand still believes in progress.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.590
And the need not just for individuals,

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:56.840
but whole civilizations to keep experimenting,

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:00.930
to keep pushing, to stay hungry, and stay foolish.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:05.405
Try everything, take nothing off the table.

